   "rank": 9341,
   "count": 71,
   "family": "hekur",
   "is_root": true,
   "etymology": "Uncertain. There are various possibilities.\n# a substrate pre-Indo-European word with no recorded or surviving cognates (words for technologies are retained, otherwise we would have expected Albanian to have borrowed the word for iron from one of the many neighbors, invaders or directly from PIE)\n# a substrate word cognate to Old Armenian երկաթ (erkatʻ), via a proto-form with metathesis *herku\n# related to hekë, from Proto-Indo-European *skek- (“to jump, be agile”), thus 'to tremble, shiver', literally 'that which trembles, jingles', with a semantic development similar to kërcej, meaning both 'to jump, dance' and 'to make a cracking sound'.\n# an irregular cognate of Ancient Greek ἄγκυρα (ánkura, “anchor”).\n# from *soikros (Gustav Meyer), akin to Sanskrit सिञ्चति (siñcáti, “to pour out, discharge, emit, shed”)",
   "etymology_class": null,
   "source_lang": null,
